<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#neopalatial-period">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Neopalatial period</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1700–1450 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century-bce">18th century BCE</a> · Also known as New Palace period</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Neopalatial or New Palace period is the high point of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/minoan-civilisation">Minoan civilisation</a> on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a>, from about 1700 to 1450 BCE, when the palaces of Knossos, Phaistos, Malia and Zakros were rebuilt on a grander scale and Minoan art, trade and influence spread across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a>. It ended with widespread destructions around 1450 BCE, after which Mycenaean <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> ruled from Knossos.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/neopalatial-period.jpg" alt="Neopalatial period" width="320" height="312" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: ArchaiOptix · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:LM_IB_marine_style_tall_alabastron_from_Knossos_-_Herakleion_AM_2690.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
